## Current state — read this before doing anything

**Prior completed project:** a raw Node.js (no Express) Animal Shelter REST
API, built to feel the pain Express solves before Express was introduced.
Lives in a separate repo (`animal-shelter-frontend/backend`), not this one.
Status: `GET /animals`, `GET /animals/:id`, `POST /animals`,
`POST /animals/:id/photo` all work, wired to real streams (`getRequestBody`,
`getRequestBuffer`), CORS/OPTIONS handled manually. `PATCH`/`DELETE` were
**deliberately not built in raw Node** — the routing/body-parsing pain they'd
teach was already learned three times over (GET/POST/photo-upload), so there
was no remaining lesson in repeating it a fourth time. They will be built
fresh in Express instead, on the new project below. Do not go add them to the
raw-Node project unless explicitly asked to.

**Now starting: Express.js, from the very beginning of Phase 5 below**, on a
brand-new, much larger project.

### This project: Marketspace

- **`frontend/`** — a React (Vite) app, already built and delivered as a UI
  **placeholder layer**, build-verified (`npm run build` succeeds, 63 source
  files). It is intentionally ahead of the backend.
- **`backend/`** — Express + PostgreSQL in progress (updated 2026-10-06).
  ESM now: `package.json` has `"type": "module"`; `db.js` uses `import pg from "pg"`
  and `export const pool`. Dependencies unchanged: express ^5.2.1, pg ^8.23.0,
  multer, cors, nodemon. Dev script: `node --watch --env-file=.env index.js`.
  **`backend/db/schema.sql`**: `users`, `categories` (with `slug`), `listings`,
  `listing_photos`, `comments` — all use `INTEGER GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY`
  PKs; FKs are integer; `listing_photos.file_path` is relative to `photos/`.
  **`backend/db/seed.sql`**: 8 users, 5 categories, 14 listings, 28 photo
  rows, 28 comments. Re-run: `psql -d marketspace -f backend/db/schema.sql`
  then `backend/db/seed.sql`.
  **`backend/photos/listings/`**: 28 seed JPGs named `{listing-slug}-{01|02}.jpg`
  (e.g. `walnut-headphone-stand-01.jpg`); DB stores `listings/walnut-headphone-stand-01.jpg`.
  **Layout:** `backend/index.js` only creates the app, CORS (`origin`
  `http://localhost:5173`), `express.json()`, static `/photos`, mounts
  `listingsRouter` at `/api/listings`, and listens on `PORT` (default 5000).
  Route table is `backend/routes/listings.js` (multer lives here). Handlers are
  `backend/controllers/listingsController.js`.
  **Routes built:**
  `POST /api/listings` (`createListing`) — WIP, still writes `req.body.category`
  straight into integer `category_id`; slug→id lookup not done.
  `GET /api/listings` (`getListings`) — paginated home-feed cards work, including
  the category-slug filter. See active lesson for what is still unread by SQL.
  `GET /api/listings/:id` (`getListingById`) — joins categories + listing_photos,
  returns `photoUrl` / `photos[]`.
  `POST /api/listings/:id/photos` (`uploadListingPhotos`) — multer +
  `listing_photos` insert.
  **`GET /api/health` was removed** when the handlers left `index.js`. It was
  already broken (`result` was undefined). Do not put it back unless he asks;
  it is not the next lesson.
  **Routes NOT built yet:** `GET /api/categories`, comments/reviews reads, auth,
  cart, etc.
  Frontend `USE_PLACEHOLDER_API = true` still — home feed uses the real backend
  only because `getListings()` in `listingsApi.js` bypasses `request()` with
  direct `fetch` (same pattern as `getListingById`). The category dropdown
  still comes from the placeholder via `getCategories()`. Do NOT flip the
  global placeholder off until other resources are ready.
  **Decision (2026-09-24):** no hard-coded arrays — Postgres only, one API at a time.
  Next after the remaining list filters: `GET /api/categories`, then comments reads.
  Auth still deliberately later.

### Active lesson (paused 2026-10-06): finish filters on `GET /api/listings`

**Goal:** Home page (`HomeFeedPage.jsx`) loads cards from Postgres. Unfiltered
pages and the category dropdown's slug already do. Search text, price range,
and sort do not.

**Frontend (learner wrote this; it is in the repo):**
- The address bar is the source of truth. `HomeFeedPage` uses `useSearchParams`.
  Keys written to the URL: `q`, `category`, `minPrice`, `maxPrice`, `sort`, `page`.
- `getListings({ ...filters, page, limit: DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE })` still turns that
  object into a query string with `URLSearchParams`.
- `SearchBar` takes a `query` prop and refills the input when the URL changes
  (back/forward, shared link).
- Price-only edits `replace` the current history entry. Category, sort, and
  search push a new entry. Page 1 is omitted from the URL.
- The listings `useEffect` sets `ignore = true` on cleanup so a slow response
  cannot overwrite a newer one.
- `listingsApi.js` `getListings` uses direct `fetch` to Express (not placeholder).

**Backend:** `getListings` in `backend/controllers/listingsController.js`.

Steps completed by learner (with tutor):
1. ✅ Read `page`, `limit` from `req.query`; compute `offset = (page - 1) * limit`.
2. ✅ `COUNT(*)::int AS total` — `::int` cast, `result.rows[0].total`.
3. ✅ Main `SELECT`: `JOIN categories`, `LEFT JOIN listing_photos` with
   `sort_order = 0`, `LIMIT`/`OFFSET`, map each row to
   `{ id, title, priceInCents, type, category, photoUrl, averageRating: 0 }`,
   `res.json({ listings, total, page, totalPages })`.
4. ✅ Category filter: when `req.query.category` is set, both the list query and
   the count use `WHERE categories.slug = $1` with a separate `params` array.
   When it is absent, the unfiltered queries run. Two query strings, not
   string-concatenated SQL.

**Next step for learner (do NOT skip ahead):**
`q`, `minPrice`, `maxPrice`, and `sort` are already read off `req.query` and
then unused. Add them to the SQL one at a time (he writes it; tutor reviews).
Suggested order: `q` (title/description match), then `minPrice`/`maxPrice`
(compare to `listings.price`, which is cents), then `sort`. Keep parameterization.
After those four work, the next route is `GET /api/categories` so the filter
dropdown can leave the placeholder API.

**Concepts covered since the 2026-10-05 pause:** ESM `import`/`export` and
`"type": "module"` (what `require` / `module.exports` were doing); a router
file vs a controller file vs `index.js` only mounting them; `JOIN` vs
`LEFT JOIN` for the thumbnail; mapping a DB row into the card shape the
React grid already expects; choosing a different parameterized query when a
filter is present; URL search params as the home-feed source of truth.
- **Domain, chosen deliberately:** a marketplace (physical products with
  stock) + bookable services (availability slots, like appointments) + a
  light social layer (user profiles with avatar/cover photo, follow, reviews,
  comments, messaging, notifications) + an admin panel. This mix exists
  specifically so nearly every topic in the curriculum below — auth, RBAC,
  nested resources, file uploads, search/filter/sort/pagination, transactions
  and race conditions (checkout stock, booking slots), idempotency, and an
  admin/observability angle — is needed by ONE coherent app instead of a pile
  of disconnected toy examples. It is not a task manager (explicitly ruled
  out as boring) and it does include photo upload (explicitly requested).
- **The contract to build against:** every function in `frontend/src/api/*.js`
  (15 files: `authApi`, `usersApi`, `listingsApi`, `categoriesApi`,
  `reviewsApi`, `commentsApi`, `cartApi`, `ordersApi`, `bookingsApi`,
  `favoritesApi`, `messagesApi`, `notificationsApi`, `searchApi`, `adminApi`,
  plus `config.js`) is a real `fetch()` call, not a stub, with a comment
  directly above it stating the exact Express route it expects: method, URL
  pattern, request body shape, response shape. Build backend routes to match
  those comments exactly, one resource at a time, and the matching frontend
  page will start working with zero frontend changes.

**Update this "Current state" section yourself as phases complete** (mark
what's built, what's next) so any future session — in Cursor or elsewhere —
picks up with accurate context instead of guessing.

